response:
Let $a, b, c$ be distinct, nonzero real numbers such that.
\end{center}
\begin{center}
\textbf{Solution} Here is a sadistic colorsymmetric problem . . .
\end{center}
Let $a, b, c$ be distinct, nonzero real numbers such that
\begin{align*}
    a + \frac{1}{b} &= b + \frac{1}{c}, \\
    b + \frac{1}{c} &= c + \frac{1}{a}.
\end{align*}
Then $ab + a = c^2+b$ and $bc + b = a^2 + c$. Subtracting the first equation from the second, we have
\begin{align*}
    bc-a^2+c-b &= a^2 + c - ab - a + b - c \\
    \Rightarrow c(b-a) &= (a+b - 2)c \\
    \Rightarrow c &= 0 \text{ or } c = \frac{2a+b}{a+b-1}.
\end{align*}If $c=0$, then substituting, we see that $a+b=-1$. But then \[\frac{2a+b}{a+b-1} = (-a-1) \cdot \frac{-2}{1} = \frac{2a+b}{a+b-1},\]which always yields $c$.
The second case is otherwise similar.

Then similarly,
\begin{align*}
    (2a+b-c)a &= (2b+c-a)b \\
    \Rightarrow 3a &= b \text{ or } c=\frac{4a+3b}{2b-a+1}.
\end{align*}If either of the above equalities is satisfied, applying this formula to $b$ and $c$ (whichever is not equal to $0$), we see that they always equal a multiple of $b$ (or $3a$) except when $c=a+b$. Then certainly $b$ must equal $3a$ and so $c=-3a$. Then $c=-a$ (contradiction).

Thus, $b \ne 0$ and $\frac{4a+3b}{2b-a+1} = a+b$. Subtracting both sides and solving, we can cancel $a$ to get $b=-3a$. Then substituting into $a+b=-1$, we find that $a=1/2$ or $-1$. Then $c=3a$, so $|abc| = 1$.

